- substantial first-hand experience of M&A integration, separation or transition delivery;
- successful experience in, or closely alongside, the role being coached;
- experience relevant to the transaction's archetype, workstream, country context or current phase;
- sound understanding of the Absa M&A operating model, governance and role boundaries;
- strong judgement under uncertainty and compressed timelines;
- the ability to explain context and trade-offs rather than simply prescribe an answer;
- effective questioning, listening, feedback and adult-coaching skills;
- credibility with both senior leaders and delivery teams;
- respect for confidentiality, information barriers and specialist authority;
- humility about the limits of experience from previous deals; and
- sufficient capacity to provide timely support at important moments.

-
  Title: The Coach may
  Tone: may
  Items:
    - ask coaching questions and offer practical advice;
    - review draft plans, status reports, decision papers, readiness recommendations or meeting preparation with the accountable owner;
    - observe agreed meetings or working sessions;
    - rehearse a difficult conversation, governance presentation or escalation;
    - share anonymised examples and patterns from previous deals;
    - recommend people, resources or specialist input that may help;
    - challenge whether an issue is being handled at the right level; and
    - recommend escalation through the existing governance route.
-
  Title: The Coach must
  Tone: must
  Items:
    - agree the purpose, scope, expected time commitment and boundaries of each assignment;
    - understand the coachee's role and decision mandate before advising;
    - protect confidentiality, information barriers and legal restrictions;
    - distinguish personal experience from current policy, approved standards and specialist advice;
    - disclose any conflict, outdated knowledge or limitation in their experience;
    - keep the coachee accountable for actions and decisions;
    - use the agreed route for any material concern that is not being surfaced; and
    - conclude or reduce the assignment when the agreed capability need has been met.

-
  Title: The Coach may not
  Tone: not
  Items:
    - make, approve or sign off programme, functional or specialist decisions;
    - act as an unofficial line manager or performance assessor;
    - take ownership of the coachee's plan, risks, reporting, evidence or deliverables;
    - issue instructions to the delivery team unless separately appointed to a formal delivery role;
    - represent the coachee in governance or speak on their behalf without explicit agreement;
    - create a parallel action log, reporting route or shadow governance forum;
    - override the Transaction Director, Programme Manager, Workstream Executive, Workstream Lead or another accountable authority;
    - present a previous deal's approach as mandatory where the current context differs; or
    - share confidential information, documents or identifiable lessons between transactions without approval.

- an assigned deliverable or plan activity they have not performed before;
- an upcoming decision, escalation or governance discussion;
- a difficult stakeholder conversation;
- a dependency, resource or evidence problem requiring judgement;
- preparation for testing, readiness, a dress rehearsal or cutover;
- a material outcome that did not go as expected and warrants reflection; or
- a lesson that may be useful to future transactions.

- approved budget, spend, forecast and resource assumptions;
- integration or separation costs and supplier commitments;
- business-case assumptions, synergies and when benefits land;
- stranded costs and obligations that are kept on;
- TSA (Transitional Services Agreement, which covers temporary services provided after completion) and Group Interchange costs (costs shared between Group teams and the country or business), service dependencies and exit timing;
- scope changes, delays and extended programme duration;
- customer attrition, onboarding constraints and product gaps;
- completion mechanics and funds-flow dependencies; and
- the cost of remaining risk, workarounds and weak readiness.

- explain what the Central Hub owns, and what stays with workstreams, functions and countries;
- distinguish Transaction Director authority, Programme Manager judgement and PMO Support control responsibilities;
- recognise which matters the Transaction Director can decide and which require the Executive Sponsor, formal governance or a specialist authority;
- mobilise the programme with clear ownership, governance, controls and interfaces;
- keep one view of milestones, dependencies, decisions, costs and readiness;
- challenge workstream information without taking over delivery;
- route decisions and escalations to the correct authority; and
- support readiness, cutover and programme closure with solid evidence.

Decide:
  Label: Decide
  Lead: Within the authority given to each role, the programme-management team can decide or control:
  Items:
    - how often governance forums meet and how they run;
    - reporting formats, submission deadlines and quality thresholds;
    - programme-control methods, including logs, ageing rules and issue routing;
    - the central mobilisation approach and the start-up evidence it needs;
    - how dependencies and the integrated plan are managed;
    - command-centre and programme-status rhythms; and
    - the steps, ownership and quality checks used to produce programme information.
Coordinate:
  Label: Coordinate
  Lead: The Central Hub coordinates:
  Items:
    - mobilising functional and regional spokes;
    - the integrated programme plan, transaction milestones and critical path;
    - dependencies between workstreams and between buyer and seller;
    - workstream status, programme RAID (risks, assumptions, issues and dependencies), decisions, actions and changes;
    - what SPA terms, conditions precedent and transaction commitments mean for delivery;
    - testing, dress rehearsals (practice runs of the cutover plan), readiness and cutover governance;
    - the integrated go or no-go pack and decision forum;
    - triaging and escalating issues from the command centre;
    - hypercare governance (the period of extra monitoring after cutover), stability reporting and programme closure; and
    - links with Corporate Development, Finance and Tax, Legal, control functions and counterparties.

Challenge:
  Label: Challenge
  Lead: The Central Hub should challenge:
  Items:
    - plans that lack enough detail, owners, dependencies or acceptance criteria (the conditions that must be met before an output is accepted as complete);
    - status updates that report activity rather than forecast outcomes and evidence;
    - green ratings that conflict with open critical-path dependencies;
    - workstream capacity assumptions that have not been tested;
    - risks or decisions raised too late for effective action;
    - changes that leave out cost, value, timing, control or counterparty consequences;
    - readiness claims that lack accepted evidence; and
    - governance papers that do not state the decision, options, recommendation and deadline.
  Note: Challenge should improve the owning team's work. It should not quietly move delivery ownership to the Hub.

Escalate:
  Label: Escalate
  Lead: Escalate through the correct route when:
  Items:
    - scope, budget or completion timing may change beyond what has been agreed;
    - a transaction-wide trade-off cannot be resolved by the affected owners;
    - a functional, legal, regulatory, risk, credit, tax or architecture decision needs specialist authority;
    - a country matter needs local approval, or affects customers, employees, regulators or legal duties;
    - a major remaining risk (risk left after agreed controls or actions have been applied), or the level of risk the business will accept, needs senior sign-off;
    - an unresolved dependency threatens the critical path;
    - a decision is close to its last responsible date;
    - programme capacity or leadership authority is not enough; or
    - the overall readiness position may not support a go or no-go recommendation.

- do not average workstream RAG ratings into one programme status;
- do not report green when an unresolved dependency threatens the critical path;
- keep current completion separate from forecast readiness;
- show the evidence behind milestone and readiness claims;
- separate facts, assumptions and management judgement;
- name the decision owner and the last responsible decision date;
- record accepted risk and any conditions explicitly; and
- update the plan and logs after decisions are made, not just the presentation pack.

- do not reverse delegated decisions because a senior stakeholder bypassed the programme;
- do not ask the Programme Manager for a separate version of the truth;
- do not defer a decision simply to seek complete certainty;
- do not make functional or specialist decisions outside the correct authority; and
- do not wait for the next forum when an urgent route is available.

- distinguish Sponsor accountability from Transaction Director execution responsibility;
- set clear transaction direction, success criteria and decision limits;
- appoint and empower programme leadership;
- secure executive commitment, resources and organisational priority;
- decide or sponsor material choices involving strategy, scope, budget, timing, risk or value;
- challenge the programme through evidence and outcomes rather than operational detail;
- oversee readiness and sponsor a clear go or no-go recommendation; and
- keep senior stakeholders aligned through cutover, stabilisation and handover.

Direct:
  Label: Direct
  Lead: The Executive Sponsor sets:
  Items:
    - the transaction's strategic direction and success criteria;
    - the intended operating and organisational outcome;
    - the principles used to balance value, risk, customer, employee and timing outcomes;
    - the organisational priority of transaction work;
    - the Transaction Director's mandate and decision limits;
    - the expectation for timely escalation and transparent reporting; and
    - the level of executive attention required at major transaction gates.
Delegate:
  Label: Delegate
  Lead: Delegate clearly to the Transaction Director:
  Items:
    - mobilisation of the transaction organisation;
    - integrated execution planning and programme leadership;
    - use of approved budget within stated limits;
    - transaction-level execution trade-offs within the approved direction;
    - governance operation and programme cadence;
    - cross-workstream coordination and issue resolution;
    - preparation of readiness and go or no-go recommendations; and
    - cutover, command-centre and stabilisation leadership.
  Note: Delegation should state what may be decided, the financial or risk limits, what must be reported and which triggers require escalation.

Decide:
  Label: Decide
  Lead: The Executive Sponsor makes or leads decisions concerning:
  Items:
    - the appointment, authority or replacement of the Transaction Director;
    - approval or sponsorship of the transaction business case and budget baseline, and any material rebaseline;
    - material changes to transaction direction or intended end-state;
    - enterprise resource conflicts that accountable leaders cannot resolve;
    - material changes to scope, budget, completion timing or organisational priority;
    - transaction-wide trade-offs with strategic or commercial consequences;
    - material residual risk or risk acceptance within the Sponsor's authority;
    - business-case protection and recovery actions; and
    - the Sponsor's recommendation into formal go or no-go governance.
  Note: Where a decision exceeds personal authority, the Sponsor should still own the recommendation, route and urgency.

Escalate:
  Label: Sponsor or escalate
  Lead: Take a matter to the correct formal forum or authority when:
  Items:
    - Board, regulatory or policy approval is required;
    - the decision changes the approved transaction rationale or business case beyond tolerance;
    - scope, budget, completion timing or risk appetite moves beyond the Sponsor's mandate;
    - a material legal, regulatory, credit, tax, accounting, cyber or other specialist decision requires the named authority;
    - formal risk acceptance is required at a higher level;
    - buyer or seller disagreement may affect transaction terms or completion; or
    - the formal go or no-go decision sits with a wider governance body.
  Note: Do not send an unresolved problem upward. Present the facts, options, consequences, recommendation, authority required and final date for decision.

- confirm the delegated mandate publicly;
- redirect lobbying back through the agreed decision route;
- challenge the recommendation without taking over its preparation;
- make decisions promptly when the matter reaches Sponsor level;
- resolve organisational resistance that the programme cannot overcome; and
- review delegation when repeated exceptions show the limits are unclear.

- approved budget, spend and forecast;
- integration or separation costs;
- benefit and synergy timing;
- stranded costs and retained obligations;
- transition-service costs and exit timing;
- customer or employee effects;
- delays and extended programme duration;
- scope change and supplier commitments; and
- risks that could materially alter the business case.

- classify each defect and what it means for delivery;
- distinguish Absa, seller and joint ownership of each defect;
- secure the data, access and approvals needed to fix it;
- agree fix and evidence deadlines;
- track the age of actions and escalate them before the next rehearsal window;
- check whether the Absa defect process can move fast enough;
- confirm that closure evidence meets the acceptance criteria; and
- update the next rehearsal's goals and the maturity you expect to see.

- customer attrition, delayed outreach and onboarding failure;
- product gaps, pricing issues and local revenue impacts;
- regulatory approval delays and local commitments;
- resource gaps, clashes with day-to-day work, travel and backfill needs;
- supplier costs, local contract issues and market constraints;
- rework caused by unclear hand-offs or repeated defects;
- local technology, data, property and operational readiness costs;
- TSA, recharge, buy-back or retained-service impacts that are visible locally; and
- delays that extend the programme or push back its benefits.

- explain how country maturity changes local ownership and support needs;
- tell apart what the country can decide locally and what must stay with central or specialist authority;
- get up to speed early on the deal, its obligations and what they mean locally;
- build and maintain a credible local plan, controls and readiness view;
- keep customer, employee, regulatory and operational needs visible alongside technology delivery;
- check that a shared plan date really means both sides agree what will be delivered;
- manage local issues, defects and dependencies between governance events; and
- raise Group-wide blockers and cost or revenue impacts with clear, decision-ready evidence.

Own:
  Label: Own locally
  Lead: Depending on the agreed country operating context and the authority the country has been given, the In-country PMO owns or drives:
  Items:
    - collecting and checking local facts, constraints and stakeholder information;
    - assessing local impact on customers, employees, products, processes, technology, data, controls, suppliers and legal entities;
    - the local workstream plan, milestones, owners and dependencies (tasks or decisions that other work relies on);
    - day-to-day coordination of local execution and fixes;
    - the local RAID log (risks, assumptions, issues and dependencies), plus actions and decisions;
    - local resource and capacity information, including clashes with normal day-to-day work (business as usual, or BAU);
    - local status, forecasts and raising issues;
    - producing and controlling local readiness evidence (proof that people, processes, technology, data and controls are ready);
    - coordinating local testing, rehearsal, cutover (the controlled move to the new way of working) and hypercare (extra monitoring and support just after cutover); and
    - handing local work back to business as usual and closing remaining actions.
  Note: In a supported-delivery context, the country may contribute to some of these activities rather than own them. Write down the agreed split rather than assuming it.

Coordinate:
  Label: Coordinate
  Lead: The In-country PMO coordinates with:
  Items:
    - country Accountable Executives, Workstream Executives, Workstream Leads and local specialists;
    - the Central Hub on the combined plan, governance, reporting, dependencies and transaction-level escalation;
    - functional spokes on standards, design, approvals and specialist support;
    - local Legal, Compliance, Risk, Finance, Human Capital, Technology and other control or enabling functions;
    - permitted counterparty contacts on local hand-offs, information requests and joint milestones;
    - local suppliers and service providers, through the accountable function; and
    - local regulators and customers, through the authorised functional and governance routes.

Escalate:
  Label: Escalate
  Lead: Raise a matter to someone with the authority to resolve it (escalate) when:
  Items:
    - a decision affects transaction scope, budget, timing, LD1, the TSA, the go or no-go decision, or reporting to senior leaders;
    - the matter needs Group policy, product, risk, legal, finance, tax, compliance or technology architecture authority;
    - local legal or regulatory requirements are unclear or conflict with central assumptions;
    - a serious customer, employee, regulatory or operational impact is emerging;
    - a cross-border, Group or counterparty dependency cannot be resolved locally;
    - country capacity, skills or decision authority is not enough for the work assigned;
    - the country and central team do not agree on what a milestone or hand-off means;
    - local readiness evidence is incomplete or does not match the reported status; or
    - an issue is close to the last date on which it can still be fixed.

- implementation effort and specialist-resource needs;
- supplier spend and contract consequences;
- ongoing run costs and stranded costs;
- scope changes, delays and extended transition arrangements;
- synergy assumptions and confidence in delivering them;
- customer attrition, onboarding failure, product gaps or pricing impacts; and
- costs created by workarounds, control gaps or weak readiness.

- explain what you own, what you coordinate and what you must escalate;
- mobilise a workstream with the right authority, people and controls;
- run an effective weekly delivery rhythm;
- report progress using evidence, not just a list of activity;
- manage dependencies and raise cost or schedule impacts early; and
- give a clear, evidence-based readiness recommendation.

Own:
  Label: Own
  Lead: You own:
  Items:
    - the workstream scope, backlog, outcomes and deliverables;
    - the detailed workstream plan that sits beneath the programme milestones;
    - workstream activities, owners, milestones and acceptance criteria (the conditions that must be met before an output is accepted as complete);
    - mobilising and prioritising functional specialists and delivery resources;
    - workstream risks, issues, assumptions and dependencies (an input, decision or activity that another task relies on) — together known as RAID;
    - functional decisions that fall within your delegated authority (the decisions you have been formally authorised to make);
    - testing, operational readiness and cutover (the controlled move from the current state to the new operating state) activities assigned to the workstream;
    - the quality of evidence used to support status and readiness; and
    - timely, accurate reporting of progress, forecast, risks and exceptions.

Coordinate:
  Label: Coordinate
  Lead: You coordinate with:
  Items:
    - the Workstream Executive on outcomes, trade-offs and matters needing executive authority;
    - the Workstream PM on planning, controls, governance and reporting;
    - the Central Hub and PMO on integrated milestones, standards and dependencies between workstreams;
    - other Workstream Leads where an output from one workstream feeds into another;
    - functional, regional and specialist teams that contribute to delivery;
    - Finance on costs, benefits and changes to economic assumptions; and
    - the relevant counterparty workstream where you are permitted and required to engage.

Escalate:
  Label: Escalate
  Lead: Escalate (raise the matter to someone with the authority or support needed to resolve it) promptly when:
  Items:
    - a decision would change programme scope, budget, timing or agreed outcomes;
    - a workstream trade-off needs executive judgement;
    - a critical dependency is unresolved or threatens the critical path (the activities and dependencies that directly determine the delivery date);
    - you do not have enough capacity, capability or specialist support;
    - a regulatory, legal, customer or colleague risk may be significant;
    - readiness evidence (proof that the required people, processes, technology, data and controls are ready) is missing, weak or does not match the reported status;
    - a residual risk (risk that remains after the agreed controls or actions have been applied) cannot be reduced to an acceptable level within the workstream; or
    - a decision is outside your mandate.

- outcome or milestone and current forecast;
- evidence completed and evidence still needed;
- top risks, issues, assumptions and dependencies;
- decision or action needed, who owns it and the deadline;
- impact on customers, colleagues, controls, operations or regulatory obligations;
- cost, benefit or value impact where relevant; and
- readiness recommendation, conditions and residual risk.

- record what is changing and why;
- identify the effect on outcomes, dates, dependencies and evidence;
- include people, supplier and cost effects;
- identify the person who can approve it;
- keep the existing baseline until approval is given; and
- update all affected records after the decision.

- resource and capacity assumptions;
- supplier costs and commitments;
- costed actions and remediation;
- requests for extra budget;
- scope growth and repeated additions;
- rework, repeated testing or extended support;
- delays that extend programme activity; and
- changes that may delay benefits or increase customer loss.

- distinguish your role from the Workstream Lead and central PMO;
- build and maintain an executable detailed workstream plan;
- keep risks, issues, actions, decisions and dependencies controlled;
- challenge progress using evidence and forecast dates;
- prepare effective workstream meetings and status reports;
- raise decisions and trade-offs to the correct owner;
- control changes to scope, dates, cost and required evidence; and
- support testing, readiness, cutover and hypercare planning.

Control:
  Label: Control
  Lead: You own the coordination and quality of:
  Items:
    - the detailed workstream plan, also called the L2 plan;
    - dates, owners, activities and completion conditions;
    - workstream actions, risks, issues, assumptions and dependencies;
    - the decision log and follow-up of agreed decisions;
    - workstream meeting preparation, minutes and action tracking;
    - status-report production and evidence links;
    - alignment between the workstream plan and programme milestones;
    - document versions, reporting deadlines and controlled records; and
    - planning inputs for testing, dress rehearsals, cutover and hypercare.
Challenge:
  Label: Challenge
  Lead: You should challenge:
  Items:
    - activities with no clear outcome or owner;
    - dates that are not supported by capacity or dependencies;
    - progress reported as a percentage without proof of what is complete;
    - green status that conflicts with late actions or missing evidence;
    - risks or issues without a clear response and deadline;
    - dependencies that are not accepted by both sides;
    - decisions with no named owner or required date;
    - tasks reported complete before the acceptance criteria are met; and
    - changes made outside the agreed change-control process.
  Note: Challenge the quality of the information. Do not replace the judgement of the person who owns the decision.

Escalate:
  Label: Escalate
  Lead: Raise the matter to the Workstream Lead when:
  Items:
    - a priority or functional decision is required;
    - the agreed scope, outcome or acceptance criteria may change;
    - the workstream needs more people, budget or specialist support;
    - a dependency threatens a programme milestone;
    - a risk or issue needs executive or specialist authority;
    - two owners give conflicting instructions;
    - reported status does not match the available evidence;
    - the same action repeatedly misses its date; or
    - the decision deadline is approaching and the owner has not acted.
  Note: Raise transaction-wide planning and dependency matters through the Central PMO after aligning with the Workstream Lead.

- reconcile the plan and status;
- identify overdue or ageing items;
- confirm the decisions required;
- state the options and delivery effect;
- identify the decision owner and required date; and
- prepare proposed actions and owners.

- a clear statement of the event or uncertainty;
- the outcome, customer, control, cost or date it may affect;
- an owner and target date;
- the action or response;
- current status and how long it has been open;
- the decision or support needed; and
- proof required before closure.

- use the current approved runbook;
- complete the task only when the authorised checkpoint allows it;
- record the actual start, finish and result;
- do not change task timing or order through an informal message alone;
- stop and raise any control, safety or customer concern; and
- confirm hand-off to the next task owner.

- customer loss or failed onboarding;
- product, pricing or service problems;
- supplier cost increases;
- extra people, travel or specialist support;
- repeated rework or testing;
- delays that extend programme activity; and
- workarounds that create ongoing cost or risk.

- confirm what you are being asked to deliver;
- understand which decisions you can make yourself;
- produce work that can be checked and accepted;
- recognise a risk, issue or dependency and raise it early;
- protect confidential transaction information;
- contribute safely to testing, rehearsals, cutover and hypercare; and
- identify delivery facts that may affect cost, value, customers or timing.

Do:
  Label: Do
  Lead: You are expected to:
  Items:
    - understand the task, deadline and required result;
    - use your specialist knowledge to complete the assigned work;
    - follow the agreed process, standard and security rules;
    - provide accurate data, requirements or analysis;
    - record what you did and the evidence that supports it;
    - update the relevant owner on progress;
    - complete agreed test, rehearsal or cutover tasks; and
    - correct mistakes within your task when the solution is clear and authorised.
Check:
  Label: Check
  Lead: Check with the Workstream Lead or Project Manager before you:
  Items:
    - change the agreed scope or required result;
    - change the conditions used to decide whether work is complete;
    - commit another team or the buyer or seller on the other side of the deal, known as the counterparty, to a date;
    - use confidential information in a new way or location;
    - change a control, policy, customer process or approved design;
    - accept extra cost or a supplier commitment;
    - skip an agreed test, review or approval; or
    - follow conflicting instructions from different stakeholders.

Raise:
  Label: Raise
  Lead: Raise the matter early when:
  Items:
    - an input, decision or activity that your task relies on is missing;
    - the deadline is at risk, even if it has not yet been missed;
    - the agreed result cannot be produced with the available people or information;
    - you find an error, defect or control concern;
    - evidence is incomplete or does not support completion;
    - you see a possible customer, employee, regulatory or operational impact;
    - confidential information may have been shared incorrectly;
    - a decision is outside your authority; or
    - the same problem keeps returning after it was reported as fixed.

- what has happened or may happen;
- which task or outcome is affected;
- the current evidence;
- the likely effect on customers, controls, cost or timing;
- what you have already tried;
- the help or decision needed; and
- the date by which action is required.

- follow the approved test steps;
- record the actual result, not the expected result;
- attach the required evidence;
- record a defect when the result does not match the expected outcome;
- explain the customer, control or operational effect;
- retest after the fix; and
- confirm that the fix has not caused another problem.

Experience Profile:
  - Significant experience leading complex, cross-functional transformation or transaction programmes;
  - Practical understanding of M&A integration and/or separation;
  - Strong executive judgement under incomplete information and compressed timelines;
  - Ability to operate across business, operations, technology, finance, people, risk and regulatory topics without needing to be the functional SME;
  - Excellent stakeholder management and influencing capability;
  - Strong commercial judgement and understanding of the transaction rationale;
  - Ability to identify critical path issues and distinguish genuine constraints from solvable delivery problems;
  - Confidence working directly with senior executives and the counterparty;
  - Disciplined risk and decision management; and
  - Willingness to challenge BAU processes where transaction timelines require a different delivery approach

Experience Profile:
  - Strong programme management experience in complex transformation or M&A environments;
  - Practical understanding of integration and/or separation programmes;
  - Excellent integrated planning and critical-path management capability;
  - Strong dependency, risk and issue management skills;
  - Ability to challenge senior Workstream Leads constructively;
  - Experience designing and operating executive governance;
  - Ability to convert detailed workstream information into concise executive reporting;
  - Strong facilitation and problem-solving capability;
  - Experience managing programme teams and project managers;
  - Confidence operating across business, technology, operations, risk, finance and people topics;
  - Ability to work effectively with a transaction counterparty; and
  - Strong judgement regarding when issues should be resolved at working level and when they require executive escalation.

Experience Profile:
  - Strong project and programme management fundamentals;
  - Excellent organisation and attention to detail;
  - Confidence working with integrated programme plans;
  - Experience maintaining raid, actions and decision logs;
  - Strong excel, powerpoint and programme reporting capability;
  - Ability to consolidate large volumes of information into clear programme outputs;
  - Disciplined document and version control;
  - Effective stakeholder follow-up;
  - Ability to identify inconsistencies and gaps in programme information;
  - Confidence challenging workstream leads for overdue or incomplete inputs; and
  - Ability to operate effectively in a fast-moving, deadline-driven environment.
